{
  "version": 1,
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "title": "Q1",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "How would you detect and handle a file that changes between discovery, hashing, and exact comparison?",
        "zh-cn": "如何检测并处理在 discovery、hash 和精确比较之间发生变化的文件？"
      },
      "answer": "A file is a moving target: its size, mtime, inode, and contents can all change between the moment we discover it, the moment we sample it, the moment we full-hash it, and the moment we bytewise-compare it. The pipeline must therefore never treat a fingerprint as if it were a snapshot of the file's current state.\n\nDetection. Record (size, mtime_ns, ctime_ns, inode, device, st_file_attributes) at discovery for every regular file. The cheap invariant is that size must be identical at discovery, sampling, and full hashing: the sample hook already requires the discovered size to compute the tail offset, and the full hash can re-stat before/after reading. Content changes that preserve size are caught by re-checking mtime_ns/ctime_ns after each stage and, most authoritatively, by comparing sizes and re-reading both streams byte-for-byte during the final verification. Because the final step compares live bytes rather than fingerprints, a collision or a mid-flight rewrite cannot produce a false-positive duplicate: the comparison either sees equal bytes at the same offset or it does not.\n\nHandling. Any mismatch between recorded and observed metadata is a per-file issue, not a fatal error. The stage records a FileIssue for that path, drops it from the candidate set, and lets every unrelated group survive. If a file already has a successful fingerprint but its metadata changes before comparison, treat the fingerprint as stale, re-hash once, and if it changes again mark it as an issue. This yields at-most-once retry semantics without unbounded loops.\n\nConsistency window. The only way to fully eliminate the race is to read through a stable handle: open once, then fstat the open descriptor (which pins the inode) and hash/compare using that single descriptor. Unix semantics guarantee the descriptor keeps pointing at the original inode even if the path is renamed or replaced, so the sample, hash, and comparison operate on a coherent object. On the sample/hash hooks we deliberately re-open by path so the pipeline stays testable across stage boundaries; production code would hold the descriptor and compare the fstat inode before and after, reporting a \"changed during scan\" issue when the inode identity or the byte counts diverge. Finally, mtime with sub-second resolution plus size is the pragmatic guard used by rsync-style tools: if (size, mtime_ns) is unchanged before and after, the file is assumed stable, and any residual race is accepted as a documented, low-probability hazard with the exact comparison still preventing silent data loss."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "title": "Q2",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "How should hard links, inode identity, and repeated paths affect grouping and storage savings?",
        "zh-cn": "Hard link、inode identity 和重复路径应如何影响分组与存储节省量？"
      },
      "answer": "Hard links are two directory entries that reference the same inode; the file is stored once. Deduplicating them saves nothing, because the bytes already occupy a single allocation. Inode identity (device, inode) is therefore a first-class grouping key that is orthogonal to content identity.\n\nEffect on grouping. Content-based grouping describes logical duplication; inode-based grouping describes physical allocation. The report's DuplicateGroup answers \"which paths hold equal contents\", so hard links should still be grouped by content when they appear alongside independent copies. But the storage-savings estimate must subtract the bytes shared through hard links: if a group of N paths contains K distinct (device, inode) pairs, the reclaimable space is (K - 1) * size, not (N - 1) * size. A naive count overstates savings by counting hard-linked twins as recoverable copies.\n\nRepeated paths. Path identity is str(Path(path)), so the same path arriving through overlapping roots, or the same file reached twice, is processed once (files_considered counts distinct regular files). That prevents double counting but also means the accounting is over distinct path identities, not over distinct inodes. A second subtlety: symlinks are never followed, so a symlink and its target are distinct identities and the symlink is reported as an issue rather than silently treated as a duplicate.\n\nRecommended handling. Key the exact-comparison stage on content, then within a confirmed group partition members by (st_dev, st_ino). Members sharing an inode are hard links: they should be reported (or collapsed, depending on policy) separately from true copies, and they must not inflate the reclaimable-bytes figure. When the goal is space reclamation, the canonical path is the lexically smallest path among the true copies, and each distinct inode contributes exactly one reclaimable copy. When the goal is logical deduplication across a namespace, the group can list every path but the savings math must still be inode-aware. In short: inode identity answers \"does this consume new storage?\" while content identity answers \"are these files equal?\" Both are needed, and conflating them produces incorrect both grouping and savings."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "title": "Q3",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "How would you choose queue, open-file, and worker bounds for local disks, network filesystems, and object storage?",
        "zh-cn": "对本地磁盘、网络文件系统和对象存储，应如何选择 queue、打开文件数和 worker 上限？"
      },
      "answer": "The binding constraint differs per backend, so the bounds should be derived from the bottleneck rather than fixed globally.\n\nLocal disks (SSD/NVMe). Latency is low (tens to hundreds of microseconds) and throughput is high, so a small number of workers already saturates the device; too many only thrash the page cache and increase seek pressure on HDDs. On NVMe, max_workers around 4-8 with a read buffer of 256 KiB-1 MiB per stream is a good balance; the queue depth only needs to be a small multiple of workers (roughly 2x) to hide scheduler jitter. Keep open descriptors bounded (a few thousand at most) by using a semaphore; on Linux, raise RLIMIT_NOFILE deliberately rather than discovering it via EMFILE. Since the sample and full-hash stages are I/O bound and ThreadPoolExecutor releases the GIL during read, thread-based concurrency is appropriate.\n\nNetwork filesystems (NFS/SMB). Per-operation latency is milliseconds and dominated by RTT, so throughput scales with concurrency up to the server's/network's limit, not the client CPU. Here a larger pool (16-64) pays off, but it is easily defeated by queueing at the server, which adds latency for everyone. Set max_workers modestly above the point where measured throughput stops improving (measure; the knee is usually 8-32 for a single mount). Keep the prefetch/queue depth shallow (1-2 per worker) because deep queues on a lossy link create head-of-line blocking and retry storms. Reduce syscall count by using larger reads (1-4 MiB) and, where possible, server-side copy/dedupe or checksum APIs so bytes never traverse the network. Descriptor bounds must respect both the local ulimit and any per-client open-owner limit enforced by the server.\n\nObject storage (S3-class). There is no fast random access: a ranged GET per sample and a full GET per hash each cost a request, and the tail read is a second request. So minimize requests: fetch the head and tail concurrently in one round trip of two ranged GETs, cache object metadata (ETag, size, last-modified) to avoid re-listing, and use multipart ranged GETs to hash large objects in parallel within a single object. Concurrency is bounded by server-side rate limits and request budgets rather than disk seeks, so max_workers in the 32-128 range with an adaptive, backoff-aware semaphore (handle 429/503 with exponential backoff and jitter) is reasonable. The queue must be bounded so memory does not grow with the object count; process in streaming batches. Prefer server-side integrity signals (ETag/MD5 for single-part objects, or Content-MD5) to skip client hashing, but never trust a multipart ETag as a content hash.\n\nGeneral principles. (1) Bound open descriptors and queue length explicitly; never let them scale with the number of files. (2) Make max_workers adaptive at the edges but fixed and small for the common case, and expose it as configuration (the constructor already does). (3) Keep buffers large enough to amortize per-call overhead but small enough to stay cache-friendly and keep aggregate memory bounded (workers x buffer). (4) Apply backpressure: bound the outstanding-task queue so producers cannot outrun consumers. (5) Measure per backend and tune to the throughput knee rather than a single hard-coded number."
    }
  ]
}
